    ✗ L12: smtplib.SMTP(host, port)  — apprise has no equivalent class/method construct analogous to `smtplib.SMTP(host
  ⚠ Remaining incompatibilities noted by connector LLM:
    - apprise has no connection-oriented SMTP primitive: it is a stateless, URL/service-configuration based notification dispatcher, not a persistent host/port
socket connection object like smtplib.SMTP. The dependency-injected `smtp` object in EmailNotifier is expected to behave like a live SMTP session (supporting 
`.sendmail(sender, recipients, message)` with raw RFC822 semantics); apprise cannot genuinely provide this without inventing synthetic glue that no longer 
reflects real SMTP transport behavior (e.g. requires guessing/fabricating credentials for the mailto:// URL scheme since only host/port are supplied, with no 
user/pass, no auth context).
    - The recipient/sender identity in smtplib.sendmail is fully dynamic per call, whereas apprise's URL-based service targets are typically configured up 
front (at Apprise/service-URL construction time) rather than passed per-notify-call in the same flexible way — bridging this requires re-registering or 
reconstructing service URLs on every sendmail() invocation, which is structurally different from smtplib's session model and cannot be considered a faithful 
drop-in replacement, only an approximation via wrapper glue.
    - Testability contract is weakened: the original design injects a raw SMTP-like object for unit testing (e.g. a mock with .sendmail). A wrapper around 
apprise can technically satisfy the same interface, but doing so requires simulating a protocol apprise was never designed to expose, meaning the wrapper 
effectively re-implements smtplib semantics on top of apprise rather than truly migrating to apprise's intended usage pattern (calling .notify() with 
pre-configured service URLs).

